<!-- wp:mbn-theme/hero-section {"eyebrowText":"Practice Areas","mainHeading":"Construction Accidents","subheading":"","overlayImageUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/img-fg-video-overlay-1.webp","overlayImageId":9912,"showCtaBar":false,"backgroundImageUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/img-hero-bg-construction-accident.jpg","backgroundImageId":11269,"contentJustify":"justify-between","contentVerticalAlign":"items-end","textMaxWidth":"max-w-3xl","showQuickCall":true} /-->

<!-- wp:mbn-theme/practice-area-details {"sections":[{"id":"whyHire-default","type":"whyHire","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"Why Hire Hastings & Hastings?","subtitle":"If you or someone you love was hurt because of a construction accident, schedule a free case review with the experienced and compassionate lawyers of Hastings & Hastings in Phoenix. Let us help you understand your legal options and your rights.","features":[{"id":"feature1","title":"Over 40 Years Advocating for Injury Victims","description":"Hastings & Hastings has spent over 40 years advocating for construction injury victims in the Phoenix area. Our team understands how serious construction accidents can affect your health, income, and future, and we work to help you move forward with the support you need."},{"id":"feature2","title":"Free Case Consultations With an Arizona Attorney","description":"We offer free case consultations with one of our licensed Arizona attorneys, so you can better understand your rights and legal options after a construction accident. You can ask questions, discuss what happened, and learn what steps may be available without any upfront cost."},{"id":"feature3","title":"A Discount Fee That Helps You Keep More","description":"Our “Discount Fee” means that we don’t charge you 1/3 of your settlement like most lawyers do. This approach is designed to help injury victims keep more of their recovery after a difficult accident."}],"photoUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/img-why-hire-photo.png","photoId":11259,"badge90YearsUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/badge-29-percent-diiscount.svg","badge90YearsId":9916,"mapBackgroundUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/hero-map-bg.png","mapBackgroundId":11254,"badgeNoFeeUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/img-meet-our-founding-atty.jpg","badgeNoFeeId":11266,"freeEvaluationsTitle":"Meet Our Founding Attorney","freeEvaluationsDescription":"Founding attorney David G. Hastings created Hastings & Hastings in 1981. After graduating from the University of Arizona in business administration and law, Mr. Hastings began his legal career working for insurance companies, eventually shifting to represent victims of negligence. Since then, Mr. Hastings has become known as “The Discount King”, nicknamed by the Arizona Business Gazette, because he works hard to save his clients money.<br>\nIn addition to working hard to advocate for his clients, Mr. Hastings is involved in local philanthropy. He is involved in supporting organizations such as Special Olympics Arizona and Phoenix Children’s Hospital, as well as helping build homes in Mexico with 1Mission.","millionsRecoveredTitle":"","millionsRecoveredDescription":""}},{"id":"cta1-default","type":"cta","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"logoUrl":"","logoId":0,"textureUrl":"","textureId":0,"heading":"Start Your Free Consultation","subtext":"Contact the discount accident lawyers at Hastings and Hastings today.","buttonText":"Start your free consultation now!","buttonUrl":"#contact","phoneLabel":"Call Today","phoneNumber":"(480) 480-2929"}},{"id":"testimonials-default","type":"testimonials","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"eyebrow":"Client experiences","heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue">Trusted by injury victims</span><br>across Arizona","subtitle":"When people are dealing with the aftermath of an accident, trust matters. Our clients consistently speak to the care, clarity, and support they received throughout the process.","starsIconUrl":"","starsIconId":0,"items":[{"id":"testimonial1","quote":"I would highly recommend Hastings and Hastings to anyone I know.","name":"Verified Client","role":"Car Accident Victim"},{"id":"testimonial2","quote":"I was very happy with the result!","name":"Verified Client","role":""},{"id":"testimonial3","quote":"I’m so pleased with my experience with them.","name":"Verified Client","role":""}]}},{"id":"documentation-default","type":"documentation","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue">Is My Employer Liable</span>  for a Construction Accident?","text":"<p>According to both <a href="https://www.osha.gov/workers/employer-responsibilities" target="_blank">OSHA</a> and <a href="https://www.azica.gov/adosh-frequently-asked-questions" target="_blank">ADOSH,</a> it is the responsibility of the employer to ensure that your worksite is safe. This includes construction sites. When construction site accidents occur, it means that an employer usually holds at least some of the responsibility of ensuring that the medical expenses and lost wages of the employee are taken care of. Yet, others may also be responsible as well.</p>","photoUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/img-employer-liable-for-construction-accidents.jpg","photoId":11272}},{"id":"commonCauses-default","type":"commonCauses","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue">What Are the Most Common Causes</span> of Phoenix Construction Accidents?","text":"<p>Construction sites are dangerous and filled with potential safety hazards. Even if workers and visitors do their best to manage and improve the safety conditions of the site, accidents still occur. The most common causes of Phoenix construction accidents include:</p>","photoUrl":"","photoId":0,"items":[{"id":"cause1","text":"Inexperienced or distracted equipment drivers of trucks, bulldozers, or other pieces of equipment or heavy machinery or smaller construction vehicles such as forklifts. Our legal team handles all types of <a href="/practice-areas/truck-accident-lawyer/">truck accident cases</a> and beyond."},{"id":"cause2","text":"Employees or contractors falling from platforms, scaffolding, beams, lifts, roofs, or ladders<br>Gas lines, gasoline, and other flammable or explosive materials"},{"id":"cause3","text":"Heavy objects and materials such as tools, bricks, stones, and concrete blocks can cause serious and even deadly injuries if they fall onto employees, contractors, visitors, or <a href="/practice-areas/phoenix-pedestrian-accident-attorney/">pedestrians</a>"},{"id":"cause4","text":"Workers or contractors who handle electrical systems and wiring"},{"id":"cause5","text":"<a href="/practice-areas/slip-falls/">Slips and falls</a> due to various reasons such as equipment left on the ground, spills, holes, and uneven pavement"},{"id":"cause6","text":"Scaffolding and crane collapses"},{"id":"cause7","text":"Unguarded edges"}]}},{"id":"liability-default","type":"liability","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue">Should I File a Workers’ Compensation Claim</span>  for a Construction Accident<span class="pad-text-blue">?</span>","subtitle":"If you are an employee and the Phoenix construction site is your place of employment, you should know that employers are required to carry <a href="https://www.azica.gov/claims-workers-compensation-general-information" target="_blank">workers’ compensation insurance</a>. The good news is that if you file a workers’ compensation claim for your accident, you can get help with missed work and injury-related medical care. You do not have to prove fault to receive assistance.","items":[{"id":"liability1","term":"You Must be an Employee","description":"<p>In Arizona, one of the first eligibility requirements for receiving workers’ compensation is that you must be an employee. If you’re an independent contractor, casual or occasional employee, or domestic worker, you usually aren’t covered by workers’ comp insurance.</p>"},{"id":"liability2","term":"Your Injury Must Occur \u2028on The Job","description":"<p>To qualify for workers’ compensation in Arizona, your injury or illness must have occurred while performing tasks that are a part of your job duties. If you get injured outside the typical performance of these tasks – even if you happen to be at work when it happens – the injury may not be covered.\nHowever, there are some gray areas – for instance, an employee who gets injured during a lunch break while being required to stay on company premises might be eligible for benefits.</p>"},{"id":"liability3","term":"Your Commute Usually Isn’t Covered ","description":"<p>In general, injuries sustained while commuting to and from work are not covered by workers’ compensation.</p>\n<p>Qualifying for workers’ compensation can sometimes be complex, so having legal counsel on your side will help ensure that you make informed decisions every step of the way.</p>"}],"introHeading":"Eligibility For Workers’ Compensation in Arizona","introText":"<p>Understanding the eligibility criteria for workers’ compensation in Arizona is vital if you suffer a job-related injury or illness. Staying informed helps to ensure that your rights are fully protected and that you maximize any potential benefits.</p>","backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"}},{"id":"mrbc77371b4ktukoyu7","type":"liability","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"","subtitle":"","items":[{"id":"mrbc7737kf0q9rghqlt","term":"Self-Inflicted Injuries","description":"<p>Workers’ compensation won’t cover injuries that are self-inflicted. If an injury is the result of your own misconduct, including violating company policy or protocol, it’s likely to be excluded from coverage under workers’ comp.<?p>"},{"id":"mrbc7737xhlvzd27kle","term":"Injuries Resulting from \u2028Intoxication or Drug Use","description":"<p>Workers’ compensation generally doesn’t cover injuries that result from the employee’s use of drugs or alcohol. That means if an employer can demonstrate that your work-related injury occurred while you were under the influence, you could be disqualified from compensation.</p>"},{"id":"mrbc7737qq8ogq8gm2c","term":"Injuries from Horseplay \u2028or Roughhousing","description":"<p><a href="/phoenix-workplace-injury-attorney/">Workplace accidents</a> arising from horseplay, pranks, or roughhousing likely aren’t covered by workers’ compensation either. For example, if two employees are pretending to wrestle each other and it takes a turn for the worse and results in an injury, that would ordinarily fall outside the scope of what’s “work-related.” This underscores the importance of maintaining professional conduct at all times when on duty.</p>\n<p>It’s important to know these exclusions because they play a part in determining what situations are deemed compensable.</p>"}],"introHeading":"Other Workers’ Comp Exclusions","introText":"<p>There are some other situations in which a worker won’t be eligible for workers’ compensation benefits, including (but not limited to):</p>","backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue","paddingTop":0}},{"id":"compensation-default","type":"compensation","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue"> Proving an Injury</span> Was Work Related","subtitle":"Proving that an injury is work-related is the most critical part of a successful workers’ compensation claim. It involves providing credible evidence to establish that your injury occurred within the scope of your job responsibilities. This usually includes presenting the following evidence: ","items":[{"id":"comp1","title":"Eyewitness Testimony","description":"Having colleagues or other eyewitnesses confirm what happened can support your account and help demonstrate the relationship between work and your injury. Their statements may solidify facts like timelines, location, context, and just generally what happened to cause the accident."},{"id":"comp2","title":"Medical Records","description":"Medical records corroborate the nature and extent of your injury. They can reveal information such as the date and time of the initial medical attention, how you explained the occurrence to healthcare providers, the type of injuries sustained, and treatment protocols followed. "},{"id":"comp3","title":"Video Surveillance","description":"If available, surveillance footage from security cameras provides objective visual evidence supporting your allegations. This type of documentation is particularly effective because it offers undisputed insights into what happened."},{"id":"comp4","title":"Your Written Notification to Your Employer","description":"You should always notify your employer right after the accident occurs. It demonstrates that an injury did occur and that it was serious enough to report immediately. This notification ensures there is a formal record of the incident within your workplace."}]}},{"id":"thirdParty-default","type":"thirdParty","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"text":"<p>The evidence listed above is just an example of the ways you can prove your injury was work-related. Each claim is different and may require additional types of evidence not mentioned. Always work with a lawyer as soon as possible.</p><p>However, this is only one of your options. You may also have the option to <a href="/">file a claim for personal injury</a> against third parties who may also be liable for your injuries. A third party may include but may not be limited to:</p>","items":[{"id":"thirdParty1","text":"The general contractor"},{"id":"thirdParty2","text":"Equipment suppliers"},{"id":"thirdParty3","text":"A <a href="https://www.insureon.com/blog/contractor-vs-subcontractor-whats-the-difference" target="_blank">sub-contractor</a>"},{"id":"thirdParty4","text":"Guests"},{"id":"thirdParty5","text":"The construction site owner"},{"id":"thirdParty6","text":"Trespassers"},{"id":"thirdParty7","text":"Equipment manufacturers"},{"id":"thirdParty8","text":"The architects"},{"id":"thirdParty9","text":"Machinery manufacturers"},{"id":"thirdParty10","text":"The engineers"}],"chevronIconUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/icon-chevron-right.svg","chevronIconId":11289,"photoUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/photo-third-party-claim.jpg","photoId":11261}},{"id":"attorneys-default","type":"attorneys","title":{"heading":"","paragraphs":[],"backgroundColor":"bg-light-blue"},"data":{"heading":"<span class="pad-text-blue">Our Accident & Injury Attorneys</span> Can Help","text":"<p>At Hastings and Hastings we understand that car accidents can cause all types of injuries. These injuries can range from neck and back pain, to much more severe and permanent injuries, or in the worst case — a <a href="/practice-areas/wrongful-death/">fatality</a>. Hastings and Hastings offers over 90 years of combined legal experience in handling all types of accident injuries and we urge you to call now to schedule an appointment to meet with an experienced Phoenix car accident attorney.</p>","photoUrl":"","photoId":0,"badgeCards":[{"id":"badge1","imageUrl":"","imageId":0,"description":"You pay nothing upfront and nothing out of pocket. Hastings & Hastings only gets paid if your case results in a recovery."},{"id":"badge2","imageUrl":"","imageId":0,"description":"Hastings & Hastings is known for its 29% settlement fee, giving clients a more affordable way to get experienced legal representation."},{"id":"badge3","imageUrl":"","imageId":0,"description":"Compared to firms that charge 33%, our lower fee structure gives Hastings & Hastings a meaningful cost advantage."}]}}]} /-->

